# site.env.example: copy to site.env (ignored by git), fill in on the login node, then source it.
# Never commit site.env, and never paste its values into an issue, a PR or a session log.
HPC_ACCOUNT='<account>'
HPC_PARTITION='<partition>'
B='<shared_root>/hpc-federation/bundle'   # the bundle, on a filesystem the nodes see; chmod -R go-w
SH='<scratch_root>/hpcop-images'          # mkdir -m 700: the rootfs tarballs and their .provenance, nothing else
L='<scratch_root>/hpcop-logs'             # mkdir -m 700: the batch logs
# The run's steps (run/README.md) also read these. Nodes are named by role: Z the zone, P1 to P3 the providers, S the
# agent's sandbox, which hosts no Onedata service. Node names and addresses are typed here, never in the repository.
HPC_JOB='<allocation job id>'             # one batch job holding all five nodes; every step is an srun --jobid --overlap on it
NODE_Z='<zone node>'
NODE_P1='<provider 1 node>'
NODE_P2='<provider 2 node>'
NODE_P3='<provider 3 node>'
NODE_S='<sandbox node>'
ADDR_Z='<zone node address>'              # the addresses the services reach each other on (peers of the shim)
ADDR_P1='<provider 1 node address>'
ADDR_P2='<provider 2 node address>'
ADDR_P3='<provider 3 node address>'
GATE_DIR='<shared_root>/hpc-federation/gate'   # gate.py, gatepolicy.py and gateboundary.py of the core (src/expcore_onedata/sandbox/)
# A shared imported tree (README, "A shared imported tree"; layouts/shared-import.toml names the variables of each import).
# One pair per imported space: the tree's directory on the shared filesystem and its manifest file, absolute paths with no
# '..' and no symbolic link on the way, the manifest outside every tree (a scan would import it as a file of the dataset).
# The tree is never written; the manifest is made once from the copy the study keeps (expcore_onedata.storageimport).
EXPCORE_FED_IMPORT_ALPHA='<shared_root>/data/<dataset>'
EXPCORE_FED_IMPORT_ALPHA_MANIFEST='<shared_root>/manifests/<dataset>.json'
# layouts/pilot-shape-import.toml imports four spaces and reads the same two names for each of ALPHA, BETA, GAMMA and DELTA:
# EXPCORE_FED_IMPORT_BETA, EXPCORE_FED_IMPORT_BETA_MANIFEST, and so on, one tree and one manifest per space.
# Optional: SVC_CPUS (8) and SVC_MEM (19G) for a service step; ZONE_NAME (expcore-zone); SPACE_NAME (run-space); ADMIN_MAIL.
#
# hpcfed.py (README, "The driver"; run it as `python -I -B deploy/hpc-federation/hpcfed.py`, from a directory of ours,
# never a shared one such as /tmp) reads HPC_ACCOUNT, HPC_PARTITION, SH and L from the environment
# (set -a; . site.env; set +a), submits its own held job and reads every node from Slurm: it needs none of HPC_JOB,
# NODE_* or ADDR_*, and it runs this checkout's bundle, whatever B says. Optional for it:
# HPC_STEP_PATH='/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/sbin'   # PATH inside every step (the whole
#                                                # environment is an allowlist); the sbin directories hold ss
# HPC_NODE_TMP='/tmp'                            # the node's tmpfs: TMPDIR inside every step, never Slurm's per-job one
# HPCOP_PY='python3.11'                          # the nodes' Python for the stdlib helpers: a command name or an
#                                                # absolute path (every step starts in /), always run with -I -B
# HPC_CORE_PYTHON='<shared_root>/expcore/.venv/bin/python'   # the core's Python on the nodes: for [hpc] core = "step",
#                                                # and required with an agent's node (down's sweep reaps the sandbox's
#                                                # runs with the core's reaper). Install its interpreter under the site
#                                                # directory (UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_DIR=<shared_root>/python before uv sync),
#                                                # never in the account's home, which every job and agent shares
# HPC_STEP_NETWORK='no_vni'                      # srun's --network=<value> on every step the driver launches; empty (the
#                                                # default) adds no option. For a site whose switch plugin gives each job a
#                                                # virtual network identifier and refuses a heterogeneous job's steps on
#                                                # component 0 ("Error configuring interconnect"): README, "The agent's node"
# HPC_QUEUE_WAIT_S='1800'                        # the seconds up waits for a held job to start before it cancels it and
#                                                # every other held job of that up: a whole number from 600 to 86400, 1800
#                                                # when unset. Raise it where the queue keeps a job pending longer
# HPC_NODE_DOMAIN='<node-domain>'                # for a cluster whose compute nodes' `hostname -f` is ONE label (no dot): the
#                                                # domain suffix their FQDN is to carry, one or more DNS labels ('cluster.example'
#                                                # is the shape). A node's FQDN is then `<hostname -s>.<HPC_NODE_DOMAIN>`, and its
#                                                # address is the short name's; the made-up name need not resolve, since each
#                                                # service's own hosts file maps it. Without it a one-label name is refused, naming
#                                                # this key. A dotted `hostname -f` is used as it is, set or not
# HPC_EXCLUSIVE_NODES='1'                        # 1: every held job is submitted --exclusive (whole nodes), so no other
#                                                # job shares its nodes and several ups at once are safe; it bills whole
#                                                # nodes, and the read-back refuses a job not recorded OverSubscribe=NO.
#                                                # 0 or unset: the held jobs ask what they always did
# HPC_ADMIN_MAIL='admin@example.org'
# HPC_BILLING_MEM_WEIGHT='<billing weight per GB>'  # describe prints the billing units per hour with it
# fleet.py (README, "A fleet of federations") also reads these; its up refuses without the first:
# HPC_FLEET_MAX='<federations at once in one fleet>'  # the grant and the scratch inode quota set it
# HPC_FLEET_PARALLEL='1'                         # ups or downs at once on the login node (1 when unset)
# The agent's Apptainer sandbox, which a platform template's [sandbox] names (layouts/pilot-shape.platform.toml.template):
# up reads each file and refuses one missing or whose SHA-256 is not the one declared, naming the variable; the rendered
# platform.toml holds the paths and the SHA-256s, and the campaign fingerprint holds the SHA-256s alone.
# The operator owns this file and fills every pin. The study supplies HPC_AGENT_SIF and its SHA-256 (the SIF in a 0700
# directory under images, never in SH, which holds the rootfs tarballs alone); the operator supplies the shim, the
# oneclient bundle, their SHA-256s and HPC_AGENT_WORK_ROOT (README, "Site configuration").
HPC_AGENT_SIF='<shared_root>/images/agent.sif'      # the agent's SIF, on the filesystem the login node and the nodes see
HPC_AGENT_SIF_SHA256='<its 64 hex digits>'
HPC_EGRESS_SHIM='<shared_root>/images/egresslog.so' # bundle/egresslog.c, built for that image
HPC_EGRESS_SHIM_SHA256='<its 64 hex digits>'
HPC_ONECLIENT_BUNDLE='<shared_root>/images/oneclient-bundle-<id>.tar'  # oneclient_bundle.py's tar, for a mount level
HPC_ONECLIENT_BUNDLE_SHA256='<its 64 hex digits>'                     # as its .sha256 names it
# The 443 mapper (optional), for a layout with a REST level (layouts/pilot-shape-rest.toml; the pilot's template names
# neither key, so a site that runs only that one leaves them out): bundle/gateport.c built for the agent's SIF (README,
# "The agent's gate on a shared loopback"), the preload that sends the cell's connect to 127.0.0.1:443 to the gate. up
# refuses a template that names it without both keys, a file that is missing and one whose SHA-256 is not the one
# declared, naming the variable; the sandbox checks the pin at every start.
# HPC_GATEPORT='<shared_root>/images/gateport.so'
# HPC_GATEPORT_SHA256='<its 64 hex digits>'
HPC_AGENT_WORK_ROOT='/tmp/expcore-agent'           # on the agent's node's tmpfs, under the shard manifest's tmpfs; the
                                                   # shard step makes it, 0700
# The study's own artifacts (optional): the directory its tool server's venv, its clone and its data manifests lie in, an
# absolute path of letters, digits, '_', '.', '/' and '-', with no '..'. A platform template (or a space's data_manifest in
# the layout) names paths under it as @study_artifacts@, so a public study carries no site path; a template that uses the
# A platform template's tool server runs from it (layouts/pilot-shape-slots.platform.toml.template names it as
# @study_artifacts@): its interpreter and venv, the pinned onedata-mcp clone and each condition's frozen manifest.
# placeholder without this key is refused at up, naming it. It must be on a filesystem BOTH the login node (up reads its
# whole tree once, before it submits anything, and verify-data and shards.py plan read the manifests there) and the
# agent's node (the dispatcher's fingerprint reads them there) see. The federation's render identity holds the tree's
# SHA-256 (never the path), which a shard step compares with the one its plan expects. Links are recorded by their target
# and never followed, so the directory must be self-contained (install the interpreter in it, build the venv on it): a
# link whose target leaves it, a special file (a FIFO, a socket) or a path that is no directory of its own refuses up,
# naming this variable. The directory is only read, so it may be read-only.
HPC_STUDY_ARTIFACTS='<shared_root>/studies/<study>/artifacts'
# Provider storage on the shared scratch filesystem, and the inode guard (README, "Provider storage on scratch"; DESIGN.md
# section 11). With HPC_STORAGE_ROOT set, up makes the federation's own directory below it (one per provider inside it),
# binds each provider's own directory onto its posix storage (/volumes/posix in its cell) instead of the node's tmpfs,
# refuses to start when its estimated inodes would pass 95% of the account's inode quota, and down removes exactly what up
# made. With none set, none of this runs and the providers store on their nodes' tmpfs. Setting it requires HPC_QUOTA_CMD
# and HPC_QUOTA_PATTERN: the inode check is mandatory for storage on scratch, and HPC_QUOTA_CHECK='off' is refused with it.
# Setting it also changes the federation's campaign fingerprint: the rendered platform file declares the storage kind
# (provider_storage, "shared_filesystem" with a scratch root, "tmpfs" without), so shards on the two never merge.
# HPC_STORAGE_ROOT='<scratch_root>/hpcop-storage'  # an existing directory only you can write (mkdir -m 700), never inside a shared tree,
#                                                # named by its real path: no symbolic link on the way (the nodes bind through none)
# HPC_QUOTA_CMD='<quota tool> --inodes <scratch_root>'  # prints the account's inode usage and quota; split like a shell, run with none
# HPC_QUOTA_PATTERN='inodes used (?P<used>[0-9,]+) of (?P<quota>[0-9,]+)'  # a regular expression with the named groups used and
#                                                # quota, matching the command's output exactly once and capturing each figure whole (this one reads an invented
#                                                # line, "inodes used 123,456 of 2,000,000"); write yours for the real output
# HPC_QUOTA_CHECK='off'                          # refused with HPC_STORAGE_ROOT; without it there is no check to skip
# The account's home guard (README, "The account's home, and its guard"; DESIGN.md section 11). The account's home is small
# and shared by every agent of the account, so a checkout, a venv or a slot directory left in it counts against everyone, and
# a full home breaks them all. A site should set the two keys below: with either set, up (and the fleet's up and add, once
# for all their federations) reads the home's usage and limits before it submits anything and refuses when its files or its
# bytes are past 95% of their limit, naming each figure that is past (never a path or a user: the tool's stderr and command
# line are not repeated). It needs no scratch root. It fails closed when a key is set and the home cannot be read (a command
# or a pattern missing, a command that fails or does not answer in 120 s, an output the pattern matches never or twice, a
# figure that is no whole count, a limit of 0). With neither key set there is no check, and up says nothing of the home.
# HPC_HOME_QUOTA_CMD='<quota tool> --home <home>'  # prints the home's files and bytes, used and limit; split like a shell, run with none
#                                                # (the home need not be on the scratch filesystem: a site's own script may print the figures)
# HPC_HOME_QUOTA_PATTERN='files (?P<files_used>[0-9,]+) \(soft [0-9,]+; hard (?P<files_limit>[0-9,]+)\); bytes (?P<bytes_used>[0-9,]+) \(soft [0-9,]+; hard (?P<bytes_limit>[0-9,]+)\)'
#                                                # a regular expression with the four named groups files_used, files_limit, bytes_used and
#                                                # bytes_limit, matching the command's output exactly once and capturing each figure whole (this
#                                                # one reads an invented line, "files 12,345 (soft 0; hard 50,000); bytes 1,234,567 (soft 0;
#                                                # hard 4,000,000)"; write yours for the real output). Capture the limit that BINDS: a tool may
#                                                # print a soft column that reads 0 beside the hard limit, and a captured limit of 0 is refused.
#                                                # The bytes are in the tool's own unit, the same for the used figure and the limit; a figure
#                                                # with a unit or a decimal point ("2G", "1.5") is refused, not read as a smaller count
# HPC_HOME_QUOTA_CHECK='off'                     # skips the home check, and up says so; the one value, and unset is not off
